"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][24]={"school_physics_en:M24-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M24-L1 – Demand Levels and Task Operators<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Abitur Practice and Scientific Reasoning<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two questions may use the same instruction verb yet require different\nlevels of reasoning. “Explain” can mean describing a familiar experiment\nor accounting for an unexpected deviation in a new situation. The\n<strong>complete question and context<\/strong> determine what must be\ndemonstrated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>The German KMK framework distinguishes three <strong>demand\nlevels<\/strong> (Anforderungsbereiche, AFB): <strong>I<\/strong> –\nreproduce familiar knowledge and describe practised procedures;\n<strong>II<\/strong> – select, connect and transfer knowledge to\ncomparable situations; <strong>III<\/strong> – solve complex unfamiliar\nproblems independently, justify judgements and reflect on one’s methods.\nThe KMK requires a focus on level II in Abitur examinations, with levels\nI and III also represented. These levels are <strong>not\ngrades<\/strong>, nor are they fixed labels attached to individual\nverbs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Task operators<\/strong> specify the requested action. For\nexample, “state” asks for a concise response, “calculate” requires a\ntraceable numerical method, “explain” requires a coherent physical\nconnection, and “evaluate” calls for a justified weighing of evidence or\ncriteria. These are <strong>teaching examples<\/strong>, not a complete\nofficial IQB operator list. For any real exam, consult the IQB and state\ndocuments applicable to that exam year, including permitted aids.<\/p>\n<p>A useful reading procedure is: <strong>mark the action verb →\nseparate given quantities from what is sought → select a model → check\nthe required justification<\/strong>. This links each instruction to\nphysics rather than memorising a verb out of context.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_145_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three columns compare demand levels I, II and III using recall, transfer and independent reflection; the task verbs are explicitly illustrative.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three columns compare demand levels I, II\nand III using recall, transfer and independent reflection; the task\nverbs are explicitly illustrative.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic guide: demand level depends on the whole task, not\nsolely on the action verb.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A motion problem specifies a constant speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Describe<\/strong>: This is uniform motion, covering equal\ndistances in equal intervals. <strong>Calculate<\/strong>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=v\\,t=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\times4.0\\,\\mathrm{s}=12\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Evaluate<\/strong>: Real data would have to show that speed was\nconstant; one average-speed value does not establish this. The third\naction requires assumptions and evidence limits, not just another\ncalculation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Write three progressively more independent prompts about the same\nphysical situation. For each, specify the expected response: Which\nquantity, mathematical step and explanation would be enough? Exchange\nprompts with a peer and compare answers without treating the operator\nalone as a fixed demand-level rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What particularly distinguishes AFB III from AFB I?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate distance for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=6.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the verb “explain” not determine the demand level on its\nown?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which two kinds of current official documents matter for actual exam\npreparation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">III involves independent treatment and reflection in a complex or\nunfamiliar situation; I focuses on familiar knowledge and practised\nprocedures.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=v\\,t=15\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Prior knowledge, novelty, supplied materials and required\nindependence all contribute.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The IQB operator reference applicable to the exam year and the\nrelevant state’s official exam guidance; approved aids must also be\nchecked.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>These three prompts do not reproduce a complete Abitur paper.\nOperator requirements and task formats may vary by year and state. The\nclassification here is a <strong>study aid<\/strong>, not an official\nmarking scheme.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L6<\/a> · Distinguishing\nevidence from evaluation<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L2<\/a> · Interpreting\nquestions based on supplied materials<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.1.1,\npp. 23–24: demand levels and exam tasks · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.iqb.hu-berlin.de/de/schule/sekundarstufe-ii/begleitendedokumente/\">IQB\n– Supporting documents for natural sciences<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Official task-operator\nbasis and supporting rules; verify the applicable exam year · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M24-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M24-L2 – Interpreting Data-Driven Physics Tasks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Abitur Practice and Scientific Reasoning<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A problem supplies a graph, a table and a short description of an\nexperiment. What information is actually present, and which statements\nwould already be interpretations?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Physics tasks with supplied materials require a deliberate\n<strong>data-reading sequence<\/strong>. Check the <strong>axis labels\nand units<\/strong>, whether the figure shows measured points or a model\ncurve, the origin and scale, experimental conditions, apparent scatter\nand finally the question being asked. A line drawn through points is not\nautomatically a law of nature. Determine whether the display is a\nmeasurement record, a theoretical assumption or a simple sketch.<\/p>\n<p>For a distance–time graph, the secant slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents the mean speed over the selected interval. A straight line\nwith constant slope represents uniform motion. It does not, without\nadditional evidence, predict behaviour outside the displayed range. On a\nvelocity–time graph, however, slope has units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand measures acceleration. <strong>The axes determine the physics of the\ncalculation<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_146_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An invented time series from zero to four seconds and positions 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 metres is displayed as a straight line of slope two metres per second.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An invented time series from zero to four\nseconds and positions 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 metres is displayed as a straight\nline of slope two metres per second.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Invented ideal data: the slope of a distance–time plot gives the\nconstant speed.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An explicitly <strong>invented<\/strong> table gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t/\\mathrm{s}:0,1,2,3,4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\mathrm{m}:0,2,4,6,8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=8-2=6\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=4-1=3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\Delta s/\\Delta t=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe associated straight line passes through the origin. This value\ndescribes the <strong>ideal model data<\/strong>, not the precision of\nreal measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Plot the values with clearly labelled axes and units. Add an\nexplicitly <strong>fictional model continuation<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=10\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen ask what one unexpected point at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould do to the interpretation. Explain why the apparatus must be\nunderstood before a real-world extrapolation is justified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate speed from the readings at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What unit does the slope of a velocity–time plot have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish a theoretical straight line from experimental\npoints.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Must the displayed four seconds imply the same speed one hour\nlater?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(8-0)\\,\\mathrm{m}/(4-0)\\,\\mathrm{s}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe unit of acceleration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A model line expresses an assumption; measured points are\nobservations with uncertainty and possible deviations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; further observations or an independently justified model\nassumption are required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All table entries are invented without scatter. Actual data include\nuncertainty, potential systematic errors and limits on applicability.\nInterpreting measurements calls for an argued relationship, not merely\nan attractive fitted line.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L1<\/a> · Reading\ninstructions and operators<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L3<\/a> · Planning an\nexperiment to obtain data<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.2.1,\npp. 24–25: physics tasks and interpretation of materials · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/1-3-accuracy-precision-and-significant-figures\">OpenStax\n– College Physics 2e, §1.3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Accuracy, precision and uncertainty in\ninterpreting measured data · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M24-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M24-L3 – Planning and Checking Experimental Tasks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Abitur Practice and Scientific Reasoning<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A class wants to test whether doubling the load on a spring doubles\nits extension. How can the experiment be organised so that the\nobservation actually addresses the hypothesis?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>reproducible experimental plan<\/strong> states a question,\nan <strong>independent variable<\/strong> to vary, a <strong>dependent\nvariable<\/strong> to measure and <strong>controlled variables<\/strong>\nto hold constant. It specifies equipment, measurement range, reading\nprocedure, repetitions, safety and evaluation. Comparing “before” and\n“after” without controls is often inconclusive.<\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>spring experiment<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=m\\,g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the weight of the hanging object and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the extension relative to unloaded length. Within a linear elastic\nrange, the Hooke model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=D\\,\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses spring constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly small school-laboratory loads are appropriate; avoid\noverstretching. Repeated readings can test whether the graph of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis straight. Its slope is justified only over the investigated\nrange.<\/p>\n<p>Before data collection, define the zero point and units. Unusual\nvalues should <strong>not be silently discarded<\/strong>. A conclusion\nmust report its tested range and how precisely extension could be\nread.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_147_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An experimental schematic shows a spring on a stand, a small attached mass, a scale alongside and labels for mass, extension and control variables.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An experimental schematic shows a spring\non a stand, a small attached mass, a scale alongside and labels for\nmass, extension and control variables.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Safe spring-experiment model: vary mass, measure extension and\nhold other conditions constant.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>In an <strong>invented<\/strong> model experiment, unloaded spring\nlength is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwith mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.100\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=0.020\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.981<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=0.981\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>49.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=F/\\Delta x=49.05\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis arithmetic illustrates the concept; it is not a calibration for an\nactual spring.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Use a suitable low-load classroom apparatus <strong>with qualified\nsupervision<\/strong>, or plan the experiment on paper. Write five steps:\nunloaded measurement, a small load, reading at eye level, repeated\ntrials and graphical checking. Identify one possible error (parallax)\nand a countermeasure (view the ruler squarely). Avoid heavy loads, high\nvoltage and homemade radiation equipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which variable is varied and which is measured?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=0.60\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.015<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=0.015\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an unloaded reading needed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two concrete safety or data-quality precautions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mass or force is varied; extension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.015<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.60/0.015=40\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Extension is a difference relative to initial length; without a zero\nvalue it is misidentified.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use small loads within the elastic range, secure the stand, read at\neye level or repeat readings.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Hooke’s law applies to ideal or approximately linear-elastic\ndeformation in the range tested. Real springs may show hysteresis,\nself-weight and permanent deformation. The figure is a\n<strong>schematic<\/strong>, not an instruction for unsupervised\nexperiments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L2<\/a> · Evaluating data\nand graphs<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L4<\/a> · Justifying\nresults clearly<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.2 and §3.2:\ninquiry and experimental tasks · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/1-3-accuracy-precision-and-significant-figures\">OpenStax\n– College Physics 2e, §1.3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Experimental precision and uncertainty\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M24-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M24-L4 – Justifying Multi-Step Physics Solutions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Abitur Practice and Scientific Reasoning<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A number with a correct unit is not automatically a complete physics\nanswer. Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>49<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">49\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone less convincing than a solution that shows <strong>what<\/strong>\nwas calculated, <strong>under which assumptions<\/strong>, and\n<strong>what the result does and does not mean<\/strong>?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A coherent multi-step solution follows four stages: <strong>(1)\nidentify the situation and given quantities<\/strong>, including symbols\nand units; <strong>(2) state a suitable model and its\nassumptions<\/strong>; <strong>(3) rearrange symbolically before\nsubstituting<\/strong> while tracking units; and <strong>(4) check\nmagnitude, sign, limits and physical meaning<\/strong>. In chained\nquestions, distinguish each intermediate result from what it is used to\ninfer.<\/p>\n<p>Technical language distinguishes reasoning from a fortunate\ncalculation. “The energy increases” is ambiguous unless we know\n<strong>which energy<\/strong> and <strong>which system boundary<\/strong>\nis intended. For an ideal lifting process with frictional losses\nignored, the increase in gravitational potential energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=m\\,g\\,\\Delta h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not automatically the actual electrical energy consumed by a\nlifting machine.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_148_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four linked panels show the problem and quantities, model assumptions, calculation with units and plausibility check using gravitational potential energy.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four linked panels show the problem and\nquantities, model assumptions, calculation with units and plausibility\ncheck using gravitational potential energy.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Physical reasoning links a model assumption to calculation, units\nand the limits of the result.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An object of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis lifted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta h=2.5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Model:<\/strong> constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand potential-energy change relative to the starting height.\n<strong>Equation:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=m\\,g\\,\\Delta h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Substitution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>49.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>49<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\times9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\times2.5\\,\\mathrm{m}=49.05\\,\\mathrm{J}\\approx49\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Check:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals a joule and the positive sign matches an increase in height.\nMachine losses have not been calculated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Rewrite the calculation as connected prose. Ask a peer to identify\nthe system boundaries from your explanation. Change the lifting height\nand check whether energy increases proportionally. Explain briefly why a\nreal lifting device needs an efficiency model as well.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name the four stages of a clear physics solution.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta h=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why would a result with units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe suspect here?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why need not\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequal the motor’s input energy?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Given quantities/question, model assumptions, calculation with\nunits, plausibility and limits.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>58.86<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>59<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\times9.81\\times2.0=58.86\\,\\mathrm{J}\\approx59\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis momentum rather than energy; the dimensions disagree.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Real devices experience friction, heating and other losses, so\nrequired input energy can be larger.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The\nconstant-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel applies to small height changes near Earth’s surface. Neither\nrealistic device efficiency nor acceleration phases are included. A\nwell-argued answer distinguishes model result, measurement and\nengineering use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L3<\/a> · Testing\nassumptions experimentally<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L5<\/a> · Defending a\nphysics argument orally<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.3 and §3.2.1:\ntechnical communication and evaluation of physics answers · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M24-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M24-L5 – Oral Examinations and Reflective Learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Abitur Practice and Scientific Reasoning<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>An oral examination concerns more than the final number. The examiner\nmay ask, “Which assumption underlies that explanation?” or “What should\nbe measured to test it?” How can a student answer precisely without\npretending to know what is uncertain?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A clear oral response should make the chain <strong>claim → physical\nreasoning → evidence or model test → limitation<\/strong> explicit.\nBriefly organise the question, define technical terms and respond to the\nfollow-up that was actually asked. If something is unknown, identify the\ngap and explain how it could be investigated instead of inventing\nnumbers.<\/p>\n<p>The German KMK framework describes an initial prepared presentation\nfollowed by an examination dialogue. In physics, §3.2.2 requires an oral\nexam to cover at least <strong>two content areas<\/strong> of the\nstandards; details are governed by the states. Practise connecting\ntopics such as motion and electromagnetism. <strong>A study dialogue is\nnot an official exam simulation.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A useful reflection record asks: What could I explain independently?\nWhere did my use of units, graph interpretation or model limitations\nbreak down? What exact practice question should I tackle next? Such\nobservations become testable learning actions rather than global\njudgements of ability.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_149_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A dialogue sequence with four steps: question, model-based response, evidence follow-up and reflective refinement, with two physics subject areas indicated.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A dialogue sequence with four steps:\nquestion, model-based response, evidence follow-up and reflective\nrefinement, with two physics subject areas indicated.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Effective oral reasoning makes assumptions testable and uses\nfollow-up questions to improve precision.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A student claims, “Doubling speed doubles kinetic energy.” The\nappropriate relationship is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}=\\tfrac12m v^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith fixed mass,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}(2v)/E_{\\mathrm{kin}}(v)=(2v)^2/v^2=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEnergy is <strong>four times<\/strong> as large. A good answer states the\nconstant-mass assumption and distinguishes energy from momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=mv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich merely doubles at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Role-play a five-minute discussion. Start with the speed question,\nthen ask another question involving electrical energy or induced\nvoltage. One person explains; the other asks about units, available\nevidence and model boundaries. At the end, record <strong>two specific\nstrengths and one next learning action<\/strong>. If a claim is\nuncertain, consult a reliable source rather than treating vague\nrecollection as fact.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when speed triples at fixed mass?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=mv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange under the same conditions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the purpose of a question about a model assumption?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Turn “I cannot do physics” into a testable learning action.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It becomes nine times as large because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^2=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Momentum becomes three times as large.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It checks the conditions for validity and whether the explanation is\njustified.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, “I will solve three energy questions and check units\nand assumptions in each solution.”<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The dialogue is invented and does not replace any state’s exam rules,\nmarking criteria or subject support. Reflective revision helps learning\nbut does not guarantee exam success. Physical experiments belong only in\nsuitable supervised teaching environments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L4<\/a> · Reasoning with\nassumptions and units<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L1<\/a> · Revisiting task\noperators and demand levels<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">study-path.md<\/a> ·\nOrganising revision<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§3.1.3 and\n3.2.2, pp. 24 and 26: oral-exam structure and content areas · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.iqb.hu-berlin.de/de/schule/sekundarstufe-ii/begleitendedokumente/\">IQB\n– Supporting documents for natural sciences<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Supporting evaluation\nand assessment guidance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
